@@s001 Closest pair of points
@@s002 dato:points,due:dividing line,ok:closest pair,spento:control strip
@@s003 Six points in the plane. I find the closest pair: comparing all the pairs costs O(n²).
@@s004 <b>I divide</b> with a vertical line: half of the points on the left (A, B, C), half on the right (D, E, F).
@@s005 I recurse on the left and on the right: the closest pair is the better of the two halves, with distance <code>δ</code>.
@@s006 What remains is the pair that <b>crosses</b> the line. It is enough to check a strip of width 2δ around the line: outside it, the distance is already ≥ δ.
@@s007 Result: the closest pair is <b>E–F</b>, distance 1.70. Cost <code>O(n log n)</code> with the sort by x.
@@s008 Closest pair of points
@@s009 Given n points in the plane, find the pair with minimum distance. Divide and conquer solves it in O(n log n) instead of O(n²).
@@s010 The idea: divide with a line
@@s011 Sort the points by <code>x</code> and divide them with a vertical line into two equal halves. The closest pair is on the left, on the right, or <b>crosses</b> the line. The first two are solved recursively: let <code>δ</code> be the minimum distance found.
@@s012 The case that crosses the line
@@s013 The strip
@@s014 For the pair that crosses the line it is enough to look at a <b>strip</b> of width <code>2δ</code> around the line: a point outside the strip is already further than <code>δ</code> from any point of the other half. Inside the strip, sorted by <code>y</code>, each point must be compared only with a few neighbours (at most a handful): the work is linear.
@@s015 The cost
@@s016 Recurrence: <code>T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(n)</code>, so <code>O(n log n)</code>. The sort by <code>x</code> is done only once, at the start.
@@s017 At the exam
@@s018 At the exam
@@s019 Closest pair → divide and conquer with strip: O(n log n). Phrase: <i>"Sort by x; split with a vertical line; recurse on both halves getting δ; check the strip of width 2δ, sorted by y. T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(n)."</i>
